John Talburt: We talked about how so much of what we're studying about information quality and data quality

22
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:21.350
John Talburt: Comes directly from the manufacturing revolution, based on total quality management.

23
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:28.080
John Talburt: This idea that, how to really manufacture a quality product that came out of

24
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:42.109
John Talburt: the fusion of the work that Deming and Shuhart and Duran and other quality experts were trying to get the world to understand, and they got a really welcome,

25
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:58.650
John Talburt: reception for their ideas in Japan right after World War II, when Douglas MacArthur was trying to help rebuild manufacturing for the Japanese after we had destroyed so much of their manufacturing capability, or capacity.

26
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:05.820
John Talburt: And they really took to these ideas of quality, and so that's how the quality revolution,

27
00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:20.330
John Talburt: Toyota and Honda and the many other now Asian countries quickly adopted these standards for how to really manufacture a quality product, how to go about the quality process.

28
00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:31.930
John Talburt: And so, so much of what we have now in information quality, our understanding, comes from that. So, it's all based on the idea that

29
00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:38.879
John Talburt: In information quality, we're manufacturing an information product. It's not a physical product.

30
00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:53.849
John Talburt: But it is a tangible product, like a report, or we'd mentioned some of the examples, like the transcripts of your grades that are produced, like a medical chart that your doctor uses. These are information products

31
00:03:54.010 --> 00:04:02.770
John Talburt: That are manufactured through programs and systems that read raw material, which in our case is data files.

32
00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:16.820
John Talburt: transactional files of the tests that you took in the hospital, the interviews that you had, the transactions that you made with the company, the things that you bought. All of these things are…

33
00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:26.089
John Talburt: Raw materials that go into a process of… and it's a manufacturing process in which the machinery is basically programs.

34
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:34.650
John Talburt: And those programs then put this together and create an information product. So if we think about managing information as product.

35
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:46.640
John Talburt: it makes sense to adopt these already proven quality process concepts that are now enshrined in the ISO 9000 standards.

36
00:04:47.110 --> 00:05:00.319
John Talburt: So, quality only really makes sense in the context of requirements, and this is also very relevant to your current assignment. So, in some ways, maybe it's better that I didn't record last time, and now, having graded your

37
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:04.429
John Talburt: Your papers, about doing the analysis.

38
00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:11.990
John Talburt: of that newbie file, maybe this will help you understand more of what I'm wanting in this new assignment.

39
00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:18.520
John Talburt: Because, again, the analysis you did was not a quality assessment of that data set.

40
00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:29.419
John Talburt: Because data quality is the degree to which data meet data requirements, or data specifications, or standards, however you want to phrase it.

41
00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:37.030
John Talburt: In other words, you need some guiding to say, this is what should be there, now look at what is there, and is there a gap?

42
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:55.820
John Talburt: Right? So the thing that you didn't have when I gave you the first assignment about the… of just using OpenRefine to look at… and I tried to be careful just to call it an analysis. I just wanted you to look at that data and see what you thought, what you noticed about the data. But you can't really say.

43
00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:13.600
John Talburt: that you're doing a data quality assessment of that file, because in order to do that, you really need to know what were the requirements, right? So many of you found some anomalies. For example, one that I like is… well, first of all, you found a lot of missing data, right?

44
00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:19.710
John Talburt: So, but the question is, is that a data quality problem or issue.

45
00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:39.809
John Talburt: if the requirements for that column don't require it to be complete. They don't require a value to be there, it's an optional value. In that case, a missing value is not a data quality problem, because it's not violating a data quality requirement, or a data requirement.

46
00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:46.910
John Talburt: So, so again, you can't really do a data quality assessment of data

47
00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:51.839
John Talburt: Until you know what the data requirements are. So in this new,

48
00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:58.819
John Talburt: Assignment is sort of halfway between analysis and data quality assessment, because

49
00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:15.689
John Talburt: Again, we're approaching this from the standpoint that we need to start with the product that we're building. What is the information product that we're building? So what I tried to describe in the new assignment is, I want some kind of a product, I want a report

50
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.369
John Talburt: And here's what I want in the report. So here are the requirements for the report.

51
00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:29.650
John Talburt: But I'm not giving you the report, I'm giving you some data that I want you to use to make this report. And based on that.

52
00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:45.940
John Talburt: you need to go back and say, well, if I need this in the report, what do I need in the data that comes in, and what do I need… what requirements do I need for it? Is this something that's going to be required? In which case, I need a data requirement to say.

53
00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:50.899
John Talburt: that this particular item, needs to be there. It needs to be,

54
00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:57.880
John Talburt: Not… it needs to be filled in so that I have it for the report, because that's a requirement in the report.

55
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.489
John Talburt: So, again, it's starting with the end in mind.

56
00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:07.980
John Talburt: You can't really do a good data quality assessment or even create data quality requirements

57
00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.100
John Talburt: Until you know what it is that you're trying to build.

58
00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:19.650
John Talburt: So again, this is a really important part of this course, and so there's no, like, fixed answers

59
00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:24.619
John Talburt: It's sort of like when you're learning mathematics or geometry.

60
00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:37.509
John Talburt: It's not so important that you remember all of the theorems, or all the types of calculations, is how… but it's more important what you think about. How is your thinking? What's your logic that you're going through?

61
00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:45.310
John Talburt: And the same thing is true here. I want you, in this course, to think of yourself as the information quality analyst.

62
00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:58.669
John Talburt: And you want to analyze things, right? So, in some cases, it may be very obvious what you need, in the data, or that may be very obvious that some of the data is missing, or it's not right or correct.

63
00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:13.220
John Talburt: But in many cases, you just don't know. You're not sure, and so you need to say, well, my analysis is I need to follow up, and there needs to be more work done here to understand exactly what was meant or what was needed.

64
00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:25.339
John Talburt: So, again, think of yourselves as analysts. Don't be afraid to say, well, I'm not sure about this because it's not clear from the specifications for the product.

65
00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:35.279
John Talburt: exactly what is needed here, and so there needs to be more investigation, or I need to go get more data, or I need to get something else that I don't already have.

66
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:41.200
John Talburt: So think of it in that way as being an analyst, okay? Not… not just a…

67
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:48.939
John Talburt: not all the answers are plain and clear. They require some work.

68
00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:59.369
John Talburt: So again, this gets to this point that I'm trying to make, is that when we talked about the seven basic Principles of information quality.

69
00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:03.520
John Talburt: We said that one of them, and it may be really the most important one.

70
00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.049
John Talburt: Is that you need to manage information as product.

71
00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:16.599
John Talburt: If you're thinking about information and what information you need, about the quality of the information, the data quality.

72
00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:23.779
John Talburt: you really need to anchor that in an understanding of what it is that I'm trying to build.

73
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.230
John Talburt: Again, going back to the manufacturing analogy, I'm trying to build something.

74
00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:32.659
John Talburt: If it's an information product or service.

75
00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.660
John Talburt: Then, it's something that's based on data.

76
00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:42.089
John Talburt: And so, how do I go about managing that and analyzing that?

77
00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:47.119
John Talburt: And the way that you want to start is you want to start with understanding

78
00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:49.200
John Talburt: what I'm trying to build.

79
00:10:51.240 --> 00:11:00.579
John Talburt: So again, you can think of your information systems in your organization as these information product factories. And again, we go back to the same analogy.

80
00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:07.829
John Talburt: And that is, we have raw materials, that's our source data, and it could be multiple sources of data. Typically, it is.

81
00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:16.689
John Talburt: We put that data into our machinery, which in our case is mostly programs, these operational systems that we run.

82
00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:22.150
John Talburt: And typically now, everybody has a data warehouse where we not only are

83
00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:37.480
John Talburt: Generating the information products that we need to run the business or the organization from day to day, but we save all that in a data warehouse that we can analyze later to see, what we call business intelligence.

84
00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.389
John Talburt: To see how we can do things better.

85
00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:45.350
John Talburt: And this is the whole idea of a data warehouse, and almost everybody has one.

86
00:11:45.980 --> 00:12:00.989
John Talburt: And then we have a design, blueprints for what we're building. We're building an airplane, or a car, or a toaster, whatever it is. We have design specifications. They're typically not blueprints on paper anymore, they're CAD-CAMs digitized.

87
00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:07.869
John Talburt: But nevertheless, we have some design and some requirements for what we're building.

88
00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:22.670
John Talburt: And we build those information products, and I call them products, but they could be services, too, right? More and more nowadays, we're building information services that people ask and go into, sort of like these,

89
00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.730
John Talburt: large language models, Gemini and…

90
00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:30.550
John Talburt: ChatGPT, you can think of those as information services, right?

91
00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:34.250
John Talburt: You're putting information in, you're getting information back.

92
00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:40.480
John Talburt: So it's more of a service than just a fixed report. You get a report out of it, you get a response.

93
00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:45.720
John Talburt: But the way they operate is more as a service than as a single fixed product.

94
00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.660
John Talburt: But nevertheless, whether it's product or service.

95
00:12:48.920 --> 00:13:06.729
John Talburt: It's going to… we're building it for somebody to use, right? If you're the user of your chat GPT, or you're here in school and you need a copy of your transcript, or your diploma, or you need to know your class schedule, those are all information products that are being produced.

96
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:12.259
John Talburt: Out of our information systems, which, by the way, constantly keeps changing from

97
00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:18.630
John Talburt: We used to be DeReeWorks, and now we're using Student Workday, and before that, we used,

98
00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:25.940
John Talburt: the BOSS system, and so, anyway, it's a moving target. But nevertheless,

99
00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:31.609
John Talburt: The whole idea is that we want to start right here at the beginning. We want to start here.

100
00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:44.519
John Talburt: with what it is that we're building and who we're building it for, because that's what shapes the requirements for the product. If we're trying to help somebody, some consumer of this product or service.

101
00:13:45.100 --> 00:14:01.499
John Talburt: we need to understand what do they need, and that then becomes a requirement for the product. Once we know the requirement for the product, then we can go back up and say, what are the requirements for the data that we need? What data do we need, and what is the condition of that data?

102
00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:09.040
John Talburt: So that we can put it through this operational system and build this information, product, or service that the consumer wants.

103
00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.850
John Talburt: So again, we want to work backwards.

104
00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:21.340
John Talburt: Really, actually, from the consumer, to the product, back through how we want our operational systems to work, and what

105
00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.239
John Talburt: What data that they need to work properly.

106
00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:32.950
John Talburt: So again, we… So much of our thinking around information quality, and data quality especially.

107
00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:44.389
John Talburt: And I go into a lot of companies, and they ask me about it, and I look at their systems and what they're doing. They're only focused on the left-hand side of this. They're just saying, I'm getting data in

108
00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:53.669
John Talburt: I'm gonna clean it up really clean, and this is mostly what, when you were doing your analysis, right? You were just looking at some piece of data.

109
00:14:53.860 --> 00:15:09.040
John Talburt: In that case, it was a list of movies, but it really… it really depends… the quality of that. Even though many of you said, well, you know, there's a lot of missing values, obviously there was, there's a lot of inconsistency, there were many things that you observed.

110
00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:13.130
John Talburt: But you can't really say whether those are good or bad.

111
00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:17.110
John Talburt: Until you know how it's going to be used. As I mentioned.

112
00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:21.480
John Talburt: You know, there may be a lot of missing values for,

113
00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:39.939
John Talburt: for example, for the duration of the video, or movie, whatever it was. But if you're building a product for somebody that wants to use this, and they don't care about it, that's not what they want to know. They really want to know

114
00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:44.550
John Talburt: You know, who are the… Producers? Who were the actors? Who were the…

115
00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:55.019
John Talburt: You know, who was the director, who was the producer? Many things like that. Maybe that's what they're interested in.

116
00:15:55.310 --> 00:16:08.429
John Talburt: That data may or may not be sufficient, but it doesn't require… it's not putting any requirements on the duration. And so, to say there's a lot of missing durations is not necessarily a data quality issue.

117
00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:13.529
John Talburt: It's not a data quality problem if it's not going to be used in the final product.

118
00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:30.819
John Talburt: So again, you can't really do data quality assessment until you have the requirements for the data, and those data requirements should come from the consumer requirements for the product or service that you're building. So you need to work backwards in this diagram.

119
00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:35.740
John Talburt: So, so again.

120
00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:50.970
John Talburt: what we learned from manufacturing, this is the reason that the Asian companies, Toyota and the Japanese, Korean, have become so successful, is they adopted these principles of total quality management, and there's 4 basic axioms of it.

121
00:16:51.790 --> 00:17:00.799
John Talburt: starts off… you have to understand the consumer's needs. If you're going to build something for a consumer, you have to build what they want, right?

122
00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:08.679
John Talburt: So, you can't just say, I'm gonna build a widget, and I'm gonna price it at this price, and I hope it sells.

123
00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:19.539
John Talburt: If nobody's in the market for widgets, or even if they are in the market for widgets, but they want widgets with certain features, and you're not providing those features, you're not going to be successful.

124
00:17:19.849 --> 00:17:27.789
John Talburt: So, the very first principle is you have to understand what the consumer needs from the thing that you're making.

125
00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:36.870
John Talburt: And then once you know what that is, you need a well-defined process to produce the thing that the consumer wants. And that means

126
00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:41.789
John Talburt: as I said, you're going to have to back into the materials that go into it.

127
00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.480
John Talburt: And it's especially true for an information product.

128
00:17:45.650 --> 00:18:02.049
John Talburt: As I said, you know, if the consumer is wanting to know certain things about movies, you need to know what those things are. And just because you have a data set that has movie information in it, it may not be sufficient or adequate to produce what they want.

129
00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:13.800
John Talburt: And it… you can't really judge how well it's going to… what the quality of it is for that product until you know the product requirements.

130
00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:22.780
John Talburt: Then that tells you what the requirements should be for the data, and then you can assess its quality in terms of being an input into that.

131
00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.289
John Talburt: Process for that product.

132
00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:35.689
John Talburt: Another thing you have to understand is the life cycle. And again, as I pointed out, you know, I mean, if you're manufacturing something.

133
00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:40.899
John Talburt: The products, and this is true for information products, have a life cycle.

134
00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:48.900
John Talburt: People want certain things at certain times, but over time, it changes what it is that they want.

135
00:18:49.360 --> 00:19:01.669
John Talburt: So you have to understand, is the product that you're building something that's an… you're introducing something early in its life cycle, where people are just now starting to want this?

136
00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:03.150
John Talburt: Is it…

137
00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:14.339
John Talburt: Is it something that's well understood and people… you know that people want it, it's a mature product, or is it something that, you know, people really don't need that much anymore?

138
00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:30.829
John Talburt: things have changed, and they don't need this particular information, and they might not need it in a certain timely way. And so, you need to constantly think about the things that you're building. What's the demand for them? Where are you in the life cycle of that product?

139
00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:35.540
John Talburt: And then another extremely important one, or at least all four are very important.

140
00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:46.899
John Talburt: Is that in order to make that bridge between the consumer and all the way back into the materials that need to go into building that product.

141
00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:57.859
John Talburt: You need a product manager, because they're the ones who keep on top and understand what it is at this time that your consumers are wanting in these products or services.

142
00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:13.280
John Talburt: And as that changes, they can communicate that, translate that back into your organization, to the people that are building these systems, to say, alright, here, we need to change. We're getting demand

143
00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:29.630
John Talburt: For this particular feature, here's something that's been missing that people are wanting in this information product. We need to add that in. If we need to add that in, do we already have this information, or do we need to go out and obtain other information that we didn't have already?

144
00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:34.280
John Talburt: So that would create new data requirements. We're requiring certain items

145
00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:46.000
John Talburt: data, we need to get those, and we need to be sure that those items, we have those items, they're correct, they're timely, all of these other issues that we can use to assess the data going in.

146
00:20:46.050 --> 00:21:03.179
John Talburt: But again, you can't really assess the quality of raw data coming in to an organization until you know exactly how it's going to be used in producing an information product or service that meets consumer needs.

147
00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:15.240
John Talburt: So, these are in here. I won't go through this a lot, but if you're doing an analysis, you're going to be a data quality analyst, or an information quality analyst.

148
00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:24.540
John Talburt: Then again, if you're… somebody calls you in, you get a job, and you, you know, then the first thing you really should be inquiring about…

149
00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:33.299
John Talburt: is not what data they're getting, but what they're trying to produce. What are the products that they're trying to,

150
00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:43.100
John Talburt: produce, and do they really know what these products are? Are they well-defined? Do we know exactly what the product requirements are?

151
00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:46.789
John Talburt: And which ones are really important? They may be manufacturing

152
00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.479
John Talburt: Are producing, you know, hundreds of information products.

153
00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:58.740
John Talburt: a large corporation, a bank, something like that. But you really need to know where to start. What are the key ones? What's the most important?

154
00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:06.780
John Talburt: And, who… you have to understand, again, based on those principles, who the users are and how they're using those products.

155
00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:09.750
John Talburt: So,

156
00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:24.340
John Talburt: This is where, there are many methodologies and recommendations for how to manage data quality, and later we'll go in to talk about some of the others. There's the DMBOK, there's the Six Sigma.

157
00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:29.980
John Talburt: There's DCAM, there's many other…

158
00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:38.359
John Talburt: Frameworks, if you will, that talk about managing data quality, but they all kind of have a different slant to them.

159
00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:47.359
John Talburt: And that's true here, but at least we now have a standard, an international standard. It's called the Data Quality Management Reference Model.

160
00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:51.590
John Talburt: That doesn't tell you exactly how to do it, but it tells you what

161
00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:57.199
John Talburt: Are the things that you should be, doing in terms of

162
00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:01.740
John Talburt: the results that you want to get, and so we'll talk about this in a minute.

163
00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:12.560
John Talburt: So, it has 3 parts to it. It has an implementation process, where you're actually doing the data quality, but then there are data-related support processes.

164
00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:16.940
John Talburt: And then there's resource provisioning, for the processes.

165
00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:21.449
John Talburt: And so, let's talk a little bit about each of these.

166
00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:34.330
John Talburt: So, implementation, you remember we talked about, Shuhart and dimming, and you remember, there really laid the foundation for what we mean by data quality.

167
00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:38.010
John Talburt: And how to, manage it.

168
00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:46.620
John Talburt: And they, shuhart came up with this idea of a cycle, a repeated cycle that starts with,

169
00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:52.220
John Talburt: planning, Doing what you planned, checking what you did.

170
00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:58.819
John Talburt: And then, analyzing that to see if it could be improved. Plan, do, check, act.

171
00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.680
John Talburt: Cycle that they called it.

172
00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:10.499
John Talburt: Shuhart actually came up with that, and Deming wanted to kind of rename it as Plan, New, Study. He wanted to study

173
00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:12.629
John Talburt: And then act, or analyze.

174
00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:14.320
John Talburt: And so,

175
00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:21.550
John Talburt: So, one of the great things about the ISO standard is that it's based on the same principle.

176
00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:25.809
John Talburt: It's based on the Shuhart-Demmy Plan, Do, Check, Act cycle.

177
00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:33.720
John Talburt: And so, the data quality planning and requirements is the plan.

178
00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:37.490
John Talburt: Data quality control is the do.

179
00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.890
John Talburt: And data quality assurance is the check.

180
00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:45.469
John Talburt: and data quality analysis and improvement is the act. So.

181
00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:56.849
John Talburt: What do we mean by this? And I mentioned this earlier, and again, I want you to come away from this course having a good understanding of the difference between data quality control

182
00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.090
John Talburt: And data quality assurance.

183
00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:11.269
John Talburt: So, data quality control is what we just got through talking about, is where we're building a data or an information product or service. So, you're building, and you have a product

184
00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:23.049
John Talburt: Data Quality Control is looking to be sure that the product that you built meets the requirements, the consumer requirements that you gathered for that product.

185
00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:33.640
John Talburt: So, data quality control is applied at the end of the process when you've manufactured or built the product that you're trying to build.

186
00:25:33.950 --> 00:25:39.920
John Talburt: So, data quality control is, does the product, the finished product, meet requirements?

187
00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:43.950
John Talburt: So that's its quality. Is it meeting the product requirements?

188
00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:46.939
John Talburt: Data quality assurance.

189
00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:55.540
John Talburt: are the checks that you do during the process, not after you've built the product, but while you're building that product. Like.

190
00:25:55.750 --> 00:26:06.299
John Talburt: the data that comes into the door, you're looking at, like you were doing with the analysis that you just got through doing on the movie file. If that's an input coming into some product.

191
00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:15.449
John Talburt: then data quality assurance is saying, well, I have now certain requirements for that data that's coming in that I'm going to use to build this product.

192
00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:30.099
John Talburt: Is it meeting those requirements? Because if the first data coming in the door is not meeting the requirements, then I don't have any hope that the product that I'm going to build will meet its requirements. It will pass quality control.

193
00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:34.690
John Talburt: So, data quality assurance are things that you do to assure

194
00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:38.380
John Talburt: That data quality control will succeed.

195
00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:43.360
John Talburt: Control happens at the product, assurance happens during the process.

196
00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:48.560
John Talburt: Many people, even many people who are working supposedly, in quality

197
00:26:48.790 --> 00:27:01.159
John Talburt: roles don't clearly understand the difference between data quality control and data quality, or even if they're not doing data, if they're doing, for example, software.

198
00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:12.269
John Talburt: Or they're manufacturing. Quality control is when you're building the pro… is assure… sorry. Quality control is making sure that

199
00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:16.110
John Talburt: Finished product meets the product requirements.

200
00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:27.609
John Talburt: Whereas quality assurance is assuring that the parts that you're going in, if you're building a physical product, or if you're building an information product, that the data that you're getting

201
00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:39.370
John Talburt: that it meets its requirements to be sure that when you've assembled that final product, it will meet its requirements. It'll pass data control. So you're trying to assure

202
00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:47.199
John Talburt: the… The process is working properly, so that quality control succeeds in the end.

203
00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:55.110
John Talburt: And then the analysis and improvement is that there's always going to be issues that you find. There will always be problems.

204
00:27:55.330 --> 00:28:00.130
John Talburt: Some that are anticipated, some that are not anticipated, that are surprising.

205
00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:13.140
John Talburt: If you think about physical manufacturing, right, you can have drift in the machines, the machine bearings are wearing down, things are getting out of… and you have measurements that you take. You've got tolerances.

206
00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:20.239
John Talburt: So, you can think in manufacturing that data quality assurance is measuring tolerances of parts.

207
00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:34.049
John Talburt: Are the parts meeting the dimensions, the specifications that you need so that they will assemble together and make a final working product that works correctly? Just think about the thousands of things that

208
00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:45.769
John Talburt: you put together in an automobile, if you're… if you're making a quality automobile. And so quality assurance is measuring all those things, measuring the actual diameters and the…

209
00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:46.940
John Talburt: the,

210
00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:59.019
John Talburt: things that you're doing on the interior, and the knobs, and all the holes in the dashboard are in the right place, and all that. Those are assuring that they will all fit together in the end.

211
00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:06.780
John Talburt: And complete, you'll have a car that passes the data… the quality control

212
00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:18.420
John Talburt: for that automobile. It'll start, the interior's clean, you can drive it away, the steering wheel works, every… all of these things. They go through thousands… I'm sorry, probably hundreds of checks.

213
00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:25.010
John Talburt: For each car that they manufacture, to be sure that it meets quality control.

214
00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:28.439
John Talburt: The same thing should be happening

215
00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:47.169
John Talburt: for information products, but then you're going to find things that don't work out. You're going to find that there are certain parts that don't meet tolerances. You're going to find that even though the parts have met all the tolerances, in the end, what you're building just doesn't seem to work correctly.

216
00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:54.420
John Talburt: And that's a failure of quality control. So these failures of quality assurance and quality control

217
00:29:54.570 --> 00:30:11.559
John Talburt: You take and you collect those, and you analyze them and see, you know, was this just a fluke? Somebody had a bad day on the shop floor, or is this something… we're getting something wrong from this particular supplier? We need to correct that.

218
00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:19.550
John Talburt: So that you need to constantly analyze and improve your quality control and quality assurance failures.

219
00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:28.190
John Talburt: So that you can act on those and to try to make corrections to continually improve the process that you're… of building this particular product.

220
00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:37.290
John Talburt: So, those have been adopted in the ISO 8000 and the data quality management process reference model.

221
00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:43.829
John Talburt: And so here's… and you'll see this if you look at the actual standard that I put in the resources folder.

222
00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:55.870
John Talburt: So again, there's the three overall processes. The implementation process, which has four parts. It's the plan. You can see there's quality planning up on the upper left.

223
00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:59.660
John Talburt: And then, that leads to,

224
00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:07.640
John Talburt: Quality control, which leads to quality assessment, or assurance, if you will. Should put assurance there.

225
00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:16.870
John Talburt: And then… then you do data quality improvement. So it really follows the plan, do, check, act cycle in the four parts.

226
00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:21.430
John Talburt: Now, there are other things that are related to it. We talked about, and back in the Principles.

227
00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:28.060
John Talburt: even though you're getting very good data, if you don't have a good data architecture.

228
00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:30.390
John Talburt: You still won't succeed, right?

229
00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:43.009
John Talburt: If you don't have the right database schema, if you're not using the right processes to manipulate the data, you need to understand what's the data architecture, how do you move data around, transfer it.

230
00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:45.400
John Talburt: One of the things that…

231
00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:53.109
John Talburt: That some of you noticed, not everyone, in this last assignment, when you were looking at the movie file.

232
00:31:53.470 --> 00:32:00.039
John Talburt: If you… some of you noticed that one of the director's names had a strange character in it.

233
00:32:00.410 --> 00:32:06.509
John Talburt: And so this has to do with the data transfer. If you look at the original file.

234
00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:13.430
John Talburt: You'll see that it's in there correctly, in the last name, I'm trying to remember the guy's last name.

235
00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:19.230
John Talburt: But anyway, it had a Spanish character in it, a grave-accented O.

236
00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:23.430
John Talburt: Something like,

237
00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:33.400
John Talburt: Calderon, or something like that. Anyway, the last part of his last name was R-O-N, but the O was an accented O in Spanish.

238
00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:37.129
John Talburt: The guy was a Spaniard. And so,

239
00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:41.359
John Talburt: And if you look at it, it depends on…

240
00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:49.049
John Talburt: what system you're using if it comes out correctly. So, for example, if you opened it in Notepad.

241
00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:55.379
John Talburt: Or a notepad, or… No, plus plus, whatever that is.

242
00:32:55.500 --> 00:33:01.099
John Talburt: You'd see it's correct, that that character is rendered correctly.

243
00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:06.950
John Talburt: But one of the things, if you're using OpenRefine, and you open that file in OpenRefine.

244
00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:11.069
John Talburt: It looks like some kind of oriental character.

245
00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:14.900
John Talburt: It misinterprets that character, doesn't render it properly.

246
00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:19.459
John Talburt: The underlying data hasn't changed, but its rendering has changed.

247
00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:28.550
John Talburt: And it actually gives you even a different one if you open it in Excel. So this gets back to data architecture and data transfer management.

248
00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:33.639
John Talburt: So that you can understand correctly what data you're really looking at.

249
00:33:35.560 --> 00:33:52.790
John Talburt: Is there data operations management? How do you overall… how does your process work? And then, of course, a very important one now is data security. It's becoming more and more important to prevent data leakage, and especially in highly regulated organizations.

250
00:33:53.200 --> 00:34:11.530
John Talburt: And then, finally, there's resource provisioning. You know, how are you managing? What's the structure, the hierarchy of management? Who has roles? What are the… are the roles well-defined of who's supposed to be doing what? Do you have a data product manager? Is there somebody that's,

251
00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:14.159
John Talburt: that's, bridging

252
00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:23.640
John Talburt: This gap between the consumer of the product and the people who are building it, so that you can always be assured that you're building the right thing for the consumer.

253
00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:31.800
John Talburt: And are people getting training? The systems they're using, people are always changing, using different types of,

254
00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:35.990
John Talburt: In technology and their, processes.

255
00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:43.240
John Talburt: And, you know, are the people getting trained for their roles? Do they know how to operate these systems?

256
00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:49.520
John Talburt: And know what the procedures are. This is especially important now in the area of data governance.

257
00:34:49.679 --> 00:34:54.729
John Talburt: Where, you have to follow certain rules about the decisions you make on data.

258
00:34:54.920 --> 00:35:10.390
John Talburt: And so you're not… can't just go in and change things or make changes in processes unilaterally. You have to go through a process that's defined in the data governance. So data governance gives the rules

259
00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:20.330
John Talburt: for how you manage data, how you can… Some people have said that data management are the decisions that you make about data, what you're going to do with the data.

260
00:35:20.560 --> 00:35:35.359
John Talburt: And data governance are the rules for making those decisions. So there's certain rules that are set about of what you can and can't do in your decisions about data, and that's data governance. And so that's a very important part of the resource provision.

261
00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:41.130
John Talburt: So this is a kind of a big overview of the three processes.

262
00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:44.779
John Talburt: The implementation, based on the Plan Do Check Act.

263
00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:48.989
John Talburt: Then the data-related support, and the resource provisioning.

264
00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:53.989
John Talburt: So again, talking a little bit here about this,

265
00:35:54.550 --> 00:36:01.180
John Talburt: We just talked about the, the, the implementation… process has a four-part.

266
00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:07.000
John Talburt: The planning, the quality control, the quality assurance, and then the analysis and improvement.

267
00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:14.710
John Talburt: So again, looking at it kind of in this analogy of manufacturing,

268
00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:34.220
John Talburt: then we start right here. We start with planning and requirements. What is it that we're building? What's the design, and what is it that the consumer wants? Remember, that was the number one thing in quality management. What is it that the consumer or the user of this product wants so that we can design that product?

269
00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:40.400
John Talburt: So we designed that product, and once we've designed it, then

270
00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:52.700
John Talburt: Again, this is just talking about what you need in that planning and requirements stage. You need written documentation, you need to be clear about who's responsible for meeting the requirements.

271
00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:56.329
John Talburt: And your strategy for improving it.

272
00:36:57.350 --> 00:37:04.489
John Talburt: And then that leads to quality control. You have a plan for building a product that meets consumer needs.

273
00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:12.810
John Talburt: So from that, you start building a product. You have requirements for that product based on the consumer's needs.

274
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:18.299
John Talburt: And you have a design for it, so now you know what the information product is going to look like.

275
00:37:18.430 --> 00:37:31.830
John Talburt: And that's what I tried to give you in this most recent assignment. I tried to give you a description. It's not necessarily complete and 100%, but it's a description of a particular product that someone wants to build.

276
00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:40.909
John Talburt: And from that, you should be able to infer some of the… what are some of the characteristics you need in the data that goes into it.

277
00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:44.500
John Talburt: And so I was asking you to look at one of the sources

278
00:37:44.620 --> 00:37:51.209
John Talburt: There may be others necessary, but at least one of the sources that you have, which is this,

279
00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:57.509
John Talburt: Foul of grades, because the product we're trying to build is a grade report.

280
00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:09.400
John Talburt: And so, based on what we need in the product, how suitable would that file be as an input, one of the raw materials here on the left, into that product?

281
00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:14.859
John Talburt: So I'm asking you to start inferring from the product description

282
00:38:15.250 --> 00:38:20.330
John Talburt: Some of the data requirement descriptions, for data coming in.

283
00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:29.939
John Talburt: So, we have the planning, we plan a product that's based on consumer user needs, and we have a design for it.

284
00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:35.089
John Talburt: Then we say, okay, we're gonna build this product. Here are the specifications for the product.

285
00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:45.380
John Talburt: And then, once we have that, then we can have our quality control. We can say, alright, did we really build the product the way we designed it?

286
00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.609
John Talburt: So that's the quality control.

287
00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:53.469
John Talburt: And so, again, you have to ask these questions. Do you have that in place? Do you have those written down?

288
00:38:53.800 --> 00:39:07.500
John Talburt: Can you automate that, or is it something that you have to manually do? And if you have to do it manually, can you inspect 100% of it? So I had this experience in working with a credit reporting agency.

289
00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:15.740
John Talburt: And they really, Initially, when I talked to him, this had been several years ago.

290
00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:24.350
John Talburt: Did… they had a really… and you'll find this to be true in most organizations. They had a really good quality assurance process.

291
00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:37.520
John Talburt: When data would come in, when they would receive data, they did a really good job of examining it and making sure it was standardized and complete and timely, all of these good things about the data coming in.

292
00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:41.530
John Talburt: But then they put it into a process to build a credit report.

293
00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:46.209
John Talburt: And they… they didn't really check the credit reports before they sent them out.

294
00:39:46.330 --> 00:40:04.769
John Talburt: What they relied on was that people would get… find out about their credit report. The credit reports weren't actually going to the people that the report was about. It was actually going to the companies that were subscribing to credit reports, like banks or furniture stores, when you go in to buy something on credit.

295
00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:21.880
John Talburt: what's the first thing they do? They ask you for your name and your social security number, and they put it into the system they subscribe to, this reporting agency, and that reporting agency gives back a report that says, well, here's the creditworthiness of this person that's trying to buy a sofa from you.

296
00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:40.920
John Talburt: And then, you know, and so the company might deny it, and the person might ask, well, why are you denying it? Or, you know, they may or may not tell them, but that would trigger them to go and write to this credit company and say, hey, what's in my credit report? I got turned down.

297
00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:52.630
John Talburt: And so, really, they were really relying on consumers to be their quality control. In other words, they weren't really examining the credit reports they were producing to see if they were good or not.

298
00:40:52.690 --> 00:41:10.389
John Talburt: They were just sending them out based on the fact that they thought they were putting good data into it. Again, it's this thinking that you can reverse the garbage in, garbage out, to say good data in always means that good products will come out, and that's not true.

299
00:41:10.910 --> 00:41:25.570
John Talburt: And so they… they were… they were subscribed to that theory that they were cleaning the data going in, and therefore, when it went through the process, it would be a good product coming out, and they would just send it out the door without any quality control checks.

300
00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:30.089
John Talburt: And so they started then implementing quality control after

301
00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:32.210
John Talburt: The review that we did.

302
00:41:32.320 --> 00:41:37.799
John Talburt: And it really improved. Now, one of the reasons I say that is that

303
00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:46.619
John Talburt: they were producing thousands of these credit reports every day. They were electronic and being sent out to furniture stores and banks and…

304
00:41:47.290 --> 00:41:52.439
John Talburt: Automobile dealerships, anywhere people were buying something on credit.

305
00:41:52.740 --> 00:42:02.110
John Talburt: Then the subscribers, these companies were subscribed to the credit reports, were getting these reports, So,

306
00:42:02.260 --> 00:42:16.430
John Talburt: So it was impractical for the company, the credit company, to inspect every one of the credit reports before it was sent out, so they implemented a sampling process. And so they started… and it was…

307
00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:24.290
John Talburt: Very helpful in improving the quality of the credit reports that they were sending out.

308
00:42:25.060 --> 00:42:33.640
John Talburt: So, and again, what they did is what's called stratified sampling. It's not a total random sampling, it's saying.

309
00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:40.739
John Talburt: I'm… I'm going to sample based on certain characteristics, and so some of the things that they were, were,

310
00:42:41.590 --> 00:42:53.860
John Talburt: looking at was if a credit report had more than one social security number in, it got tagged for, being a possible… there's more likely that there could be errors in it.

311
00:42:53.970 --> 00:42:58.600
John Talburt: Or if somebody had changed addresses more than 2 or 3 times.

312
00:42:58.850 --> 00:43:11.079
John Talburt: Then these were all triggers to say, these are the ones that are higher priority that we should sample from, because these are the ones that, in the past, have proved to be more problematic in terms of errors.

313
00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:16.940
John Talburt: So then, that gets us to quality assurance.

314
00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:25.380
John Talburt: that's going back into the process, saying, based on the requirements I have for my information product, our service.

315
00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:35.970
John Talburt: what do I need to be careful about in terms of the… what are the requirements that I need for the raw materials and the systems or the processes that I'm using to build that product?

316
00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:41.460
John Talburt: And that's basically, in a manufacturing, that would be measuring tolerances.

317
00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:48.830
John Talburt: In an information product, we measure them in terms of data quality dimensions that we've talked about earlier.

318
00:43:49.150 --> 00:43:55.959
John Talburt: There are many dimensional frameworks, but almost all of them are based around certain things, such as,

319
00:43:56.070 --> 00:43:59.650
John Talburt: The accuracy of the data, the completeness of the data.

320
00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:05.520
John Talburt: The consistency, either of the representation of the data, the timeliness of the data.

321
00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:09.529
John Talburt: There are other dimensions, too, that have been neglected in the past.

322
00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:12.800
John Talburt: The ones that we're talking about here are things like

323
00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:17.700
John Talburt: the validity, the consistency of the data. Is everything spelled

324
00:44:17.840 --> 00:44:30.360
John Talburt: you know, if I have 10 options for a value, and I know what those options are, what if I get something that's not one of those 10 values? Is it something that I can correct or standardize?

325
00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:44.569
John Talburt: These are all automated type things. I can… validity is one, a big go-to. Another one is completeness, right? I can detect null and blank values. Not quite always as easy to detect placeholder values.

326
00:44:44.680 --> 00:44:51.680
John Talburt: But at least non-null and blank values I can detect. So those are automated checks.

327
00:44:52.110 --> 00:45:03.610
John Talburt: But there are some very important ones, especially now in the age of AI, that are not as easy to automate and require more work. Things like objectivity. Do I have bias in the data?

328
00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:08.700
John Talburt: What about the reputation of the data? So, there are many things that can…

329
00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:21.869
John Talburt: that are… they're qualities of the data that we need to know about, but they're not easily programmed into a rule-based programming system. They require some other types of investigations, like surveys.

330
00:45:22.310 --> 00:45:23.980
John Talburt: Or interviews.

331
00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:33.519
John Talburt: And so, this is one of the obstacles in adapting the manufacturing model to information quality.

332
00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:42.110
John Talburt: We say there's a nice analogy here, we've got raw materials that are source data, we've got machinery that's programs, we're building products.

333
00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:46.119
John Talburt: But, in real manufacturing,

334
00:45:46.170 --> 00:46:03.420
John Talburt: the tolerances are really well understood, right? We know how to measure diameter, we know how to measure density or tensile strength, all these kinds of things that are pretty well clear to most people, and fairly easy to do.

335
00:46:03.470 --> 00:46:09.799
John Talburt: But when it comes to data, there's a lot of intangible things, or what we call anti-kinesthetic.

336
00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:12.500
John Talburt: They're not something that…

337
00:46:12.710 --> 00:46:19.089
John Talburt: we can touch, right? It's not something we can put a measuring tape or a caliper on to measure.

338
00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:22.650
John Talburt: And so, it becomes a little more difficult

339
00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:27.349
John Talburt: And this was a big stumbling block in the early days of information quality.

340
00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:39.240
John Talburt: bringing it into a discipline is how do you measure those tolerances? What are the analogies of measurement tolerances in data? And that's what we developed these data

341
00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:45.480
John Talburt: quality dimensional frameworks, like the Wangstrong that we mentioned, the 16 dimensions.

342
00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:52.209
John Talburt: And again, there are many of those dimensions that people have ignored up until now in the age of AI.

343
00:46:52.450 --> 00:46:57.260
John Talburt: The things like the, reputation and the,

344
00:46:57.480 --> 00:47:03.709
John Talburt: The objectivity and usefulness of the data, the relevance, things like that.

345
00:47:03.880 --> 00:47:13.270
John Talburt: that were not easily put into rules that you could put in an informatic data quality system or an IBM quality system.

346
00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:22.140
John Talburt: They were often ignored, but now they've become very important, and so now we're looking at ways to try to measure all of these dimensions.

347
00:47:24.360 --> 00:47:32.490
John Talburt: And then, again, if you need… you're going to look at your process. Do you really have those QA processes in place?

348
00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:44.979
John Talburt: And again, the same question, can they all be automated, or some of them need to be manual? And if they're manual, do you need to sample? Are you… is it impossible to

349
00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:48.569
John Talburt: Manually inspect every one of the products.

350
00:47:49.540 --> 00:47:55.189
John Talburt: So again, we… I come back to this, and again, I hope you'll remember this.

351
00:47:55.630 --> 00:48:00.800
John Talburt: Quality control and quality assurance have different meanings, and they're not interchangeable.

352
00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:15.990
John Talburt: So try to use them properly. Again, quality control applies to the finished product, whether that's an information product or a manufactured product. Is it meeting the requirements that you set up based on the consumer's needs?

353
00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:21.119
John Talburt: Quality assurance only applies to the process of building a product.

354
00:48:21.310 --> 00:48:29.369
John Talburt: Do the product components and building process meet the requirements to get you to a product that will pass quality control?

355
00:48:30.490 --> 00:48:35.899
John Talburt: So finally, the fourth part of the process is we've started with

356
00:48:36.040 --> 00:48:38.179
John Talburt: What is it we're trying to build?

357
00:48:38.300 --> 00:48:51.470
John Talburt: Then, once we know what it is, what are going to be the requirements for that product that we can check to be sure we built the right product? That's the quality control. Then we back into the system for building it, and we have quality assurance.

358
00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:54.750
John Talburt: But then the last thing is, when we have failures.

359
00:48:56.260 --> 00:49:02.709
John Talburt: And we will. There will always be some, some issues. Sometimes they're just inescapable.

360
00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:05.800
John Talburt: things that happen.

361
00:49:05.930 --> 00:49:14.830
John Talburt: But we want to analyze and be sure, was it just a one-time thing? Was it an accident? Was it a fluke? It's probably not going to happen again. Or is there something…

362
00:49:15.060 --> 00:49:31.549
John Talburt: endemic to this process, or to the data that we're getting, or somewhere in the system, is there something that's causing this to happen over and over again? And if it is, can we go back and address it and fix it so that

363
00:49:31.790 --> 00:49:37.209
John Talburt: We don't have… we can start eliminating those failures, or at least reducing them.

364
00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:44.439
John Talburt: So that, again, we have less and less effort that we have to spend, when we finally get to the product.

365
00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:56.110
John Talburt: Because once we get to the product and it's not meeting requirements, then we either have to put it back in to fix it, which takes time and energy, or we have to discard that product.

366
00:49:56.240 --> 00:50:01.939
John Talburt: And in which case, we've lost all the effort that we put into building that particular product.

367
00:50:02.370 --> 00:50:09.910
John Talburt: So, so it's important that we try to get to the best products.

368
00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:15.210
John Talburt: That are… that meet all of the quality control requirements as much as possible.

369
00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:22.010
John Talburt: I mean, our goal would be 100%, but again, we have to keep checking, because things always go wrong.

370
00:50:22.160 --> 00:50:27.950
John Talburt: And we need to constantly try to improve our processes. So this is…

371
00:50:28.130 --> 00:50:42.859
John Talburt: again, you can't do that unless you systematically collect those issues. So, another problem I find in many organizations is, yes, they'll find a data quality problem while they're doing the process, and they'll make a ticket and say, oh.

372
00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:50.130
John Talburt: okay, this needs to be fixed, we can't send this out the way it is, and so they'll immediately jump on it and fix it. But then.

373
00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:54.720
John Talburt: They don't really collect in a database all of those tickets.

374
00:50:54.900 --> 00:51:05.779
John Talburt: So that they can periodically go back, say, at the end of the month, and say, let's look at all of our problems that we had, all the tickets that we registered, and see if there's some kind of pattern in there.

375
00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:13.820
John Talburt: That we could, are there some systematic quality control or quality assurance failures that we could improve the process?

376
00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:28.559
John Talburt: So, we can look for the root causes of any kind of persistence. So, one thing is trying to sort out, is this a persistent issue? If it's a persistent issue, is it an important one? How often does it occur?

377
00:51:28.600 --> 00:51:41.159
John Talburt: And then we'd make a decision, say, okay, we're going to try to address that particular issue and fix it. And then we'd continually do that over time so that we'd try to continually improve our process.

378
00:51:42.880 --> 00:51:51.720
John Talburt: So that we can implement those processes back into our system and continue to make it better. So those are things that…

379
00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:54.790
John Talburt: We,

380
00:51:55.830 --> 00:52:07.659
John Talburt: we really can't say that we have some kind of a quality process, whether this is information quality or manufacturing quality, if we don't have these things. We have to have

381
00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:11.310
John Talburt: A plan and quality control requirements.

382
00:52:12.040 --> 00:52:15.490
John Talburt: That should lead to quality assurance requirements.

383
00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:20.740
John Talburt: And then, the other thing is that we need to collect those failures

384
00:52:20.860 --> 00:52:36.969
John Talburt: And always try to find root causes of problems, if we can, to continually improve. So, if we don't have those things, if we're not… we don't have requirements, and we're checking for those requirements, whether they're quality control or quality assurance requirements.

385
00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:44.640
John Talburt: And then we're collect… and we're not collecting failures. We can't say that we have a quality system.

386
00:52:45.650 --> 00:52:52.360
John Talburt: And many quality management systems that I've looked at, or especially data quality management.

387
00:52:52.670 --> 00:52:55.109
John Talburt: Are lacking one or more of those things.

388
00:52:55.580 --> 00:52:59.399
John Talburt: And so they're really not doing data quality management.

389
00:53:02.380 --> 00:53:06.649
John Talburt: So again, once you find these things, you have to decide what you're going to…

390
00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:12.279
John Talburt: Try to fix. What's the cost versus the benefit of these improvements.

391
00:53:12.720 --> 00:53:15.859
John Talburt: And then a thing that's often missed is

392
00:53:16.340 --> 00:53:20.760
John Talburt: Communicating when you do fix things and make things better and bragging about it.

393
00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:30.989
John Talburt: So, that brings us to the other, the data-related support processes that we talked about, and that's the data architecture, that's very important.

394
00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:34.760
John Talburt: We may have all the right data that we need to build

395
00:53:35.070 --> 00:53:39.450
John Talburt: An information product that meets consumers' needs.

396
00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:48.999
John Talburt: But if we don't architect the system for building it, for storing the data, representing the data, we don't have the right database schema, we're not using the right

397
00:53:49.190 --> 00:53:52.860
John Talburt: Programs were not using the right transformations of the data.

398
00:53:52.970 --> 00:54:00.910
John Talburt: Then it will fail. So… so understanding the data architecture that we're using is very important. That has…

399
00:54:01.070 --> 00:54:03.270
John Talburt: We have to look at the quality of that.

400
00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:17.360
John Talburt: the data transfer, as I was talking about, if you move data around, there's always the chance of losing some of it or changing it, just like the character issue that I just pointed out in that little example that you did with the movies.

401
00:54:17.630 --> 00:54:24.410
John Talburt: You'll find that there's a problem in transferring data between your desktop system

402
00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:27.850
John Talburt: I gave it to you in a CSV format.

403
00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:37.000
John Talburt: that if you look at… it was in a… in what we call a UTF-8, coded characters.

404
00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:38.350
John Talburt: And then…

405
00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:52.149
John Talburt: those… that's rendered correctly in most of your systems, but for some reason, OpenRefine did not render that. So when I transferred that data into OpenRefine, into another system, things changed.

406
00:54:52.450 --> 00:54:59.779
John Talburt: And it, and it looked like it was incorrect, but really, in the, in the, in the base data, it was rendered

407
00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:16.119
John Talburt: or it was represented correctly, it just wasn't rendered correctly in certain other systems. So somebody might see that and flag that as bad data, or… but again, it's a reflection of the actual architectures and systems that you're using.

408
00:55:16.770 --> 00:55:23.529
John Talburt: And then data operations overall, are you efficiently managing the systems, doing things in the right order?

409
00:55:23.640 --> 00:55:26.350
John Talburt: And being as efficient as possible.

410
00:55:26.500 --> 00:55:29.290
John Talburt: To get your products built.

411
00:55:29.880 --> 00:55:40.319
John Talburt: And then, last but not least, is the data security. Do the right people have access? Everybody that needs to use the data needs access to it, but you don't want any more than that.

412
00:55:40.550 --> 00:55:47.640
John Talburt: You don't want the data to be just accessible to anyone, especially if it has

413
00:55:47.970 --> 00:55:58.919
John Talburt: any type of personally identifiable information. Information is protected through regulation, some type of financial information.

414
00:55:59.090 --> 00:56:13.539
John Talburt: things like that. You want to be sure that you have that balance between people that need to have it can get to it, but people that shouldn't be seen can't get to it. And that's the whole idea of cybersecurity.

415
00:56:16.100 --> 00:56:21.159
John Talburt: And then finally, the third part is the resource provisioning process.

416
00:56:21.390 --> 00:56:30.749
John Talburt: Where, you really have a data quality organization management, where you have defined roles, because again, data quality management

417
00:56:30.990 --> 00:56:38.940
John Talburt: Should be a program versus a particular, process… I'm sorry, a project.

418
00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:53.669
John Talburt: The difference between a project and a program is that in a project, you… it has a definite start and end time, and a set of objectives that you're going to accomplish by the end of it.

419
00:56:54.140 --> 00:57:01.549
John Talburt: Whereas in a program, it's an ongoing process that you're running all the time, right? So data quality management

420
00:57:02.110 --> 00:57:12.459
John Talburt: you will have projects in it. In other words, suppose, like we were talking about, you've identified some root cause of a problem in your system through analyzing your failure tickets.

421
00:57:13.180 --> 00:57:26.320
John Talburt: you would launch a project to say, I'm going to fix that particular project. I'm starting now, on this time, I plan to be finished in 3 months with this, here's what I plan to accomplish. That would be a project.

422
00:57:26.850 --> 00:57:30.679
John Talburt: And so I would do that project to improve my overall

423
00:57:30.720 --> 00:57:47.089
John Talburt: program, but it's a program in the sense that it has management. I have a director for data quality management, I have data stewards that have certain roles. I have defined roles for people that are ongoing. It's not something that we do on a

424
00:57:47.090 --> 00:57:51.170
John Talburt: time-check basis. It's something that we do, continually.

425
00:57:51.880 --> 00:57:58.930
John Talburt: And, of course, everyone needs to be trained for the role that they have, within this organizational management.

426
00:57:59.710 --> 00:58:05.549
John Talburt: And again, this is something I… we… I saw this for a while, and I see it less now.

427
00:58:06.350 --> 00:58:13.780
John Talburt: Is, is people having formally defined data quality, Organizations or programs.

428
00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:16.720
John Talburt: Where they have a director, or…

429
00:58:16.850 --> 00:58:27.079
John Talburt: manager, however you want to call it, of information or data quality, and roses. A lot of this has kind of been absorbed in data governance.

430
00:58:27.310 --> 00:58:31.950
John Talburt: Typically, data quality is one of the parts of data governance.

431
00:58:32.190 --> 00:58:37.219
John Talburt: And so, but oftentimes it's not set aside very specifically.

432
00:58:37.460 --> 00:58:49.929
John Talburt: With a specific set of requirements, like having, quality control requirements documented, having quality assurance requirements documented, things like this, having a,

433
00:58:50.220 --> 00:58:56.350
John Talburt: A review process, collecting, your…

434
00:58:57.510 --> 00:59:03.060
John Talburt: collecting your data here, I'm sorry, your quality control, quality assurance requirements, and so forth.

435
00:59:03.250 --> 00:59:11.530
John Talburt: And so often that kind of gets swept under the rug, but it really needs to be visible, whether you're putting it as a separate

436
00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:22.460
John Talburt: Organization, you have a data quality management organization, or if it's umbrellaed under data governance, it still needs to have its own set of standards.

437
00:59:23.740 --> 00:59:26.680
John Talburt: So this,

438
00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:33.249
John Talburt: If you really want to comply with this standard, the ISO 8061 standard that we talked about.

439
00:59:33.820 --> 00:59:38.009
John Talburt: And again, the full documentation, I put in the resources.

440
00:59:38.410 --> 00:59:44.380
John Talburt: It's really compliance as you do a self-study, you document what you're doing.

441
00:59:44.620 --> 00:59:47.109
John Talburt: That's identified in the standard.

442
00:59:47.620 --> 00:59:55.780
John Talburt: And so, there's activities that should be performed and outcomes that should be achieved, and these are defined

443
00:59:55.890 --> 01:00:04.349
John Talburt: In the standard, so there's 51 activities that you should be undertaking in your data quality management, and you should be obtaining 82 outcomes.

444
01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:07.889
John Talburt: And so part of compliance is to document

445
01:00:08.230 --> 01:00:20.269
John Talburt: that… investigate, you know, study, do a… like, you would do a self-study of your organization, and show what you're doing. Are you doing these 51 activities?

446
01:00:20.450 --> 01:00:25.700
John Talburt: If you are, what outcomes are you getting? And so you need to document all those.

447
01:00:26.310 --> 01:00:31.070
John Talburt: And then, overall, the extent to which you're improving data quality.

448
01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:34.720
John Talburt: And then back to this, support idea.

449
01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:40.399
John Talburt: Do you really have assigned roles and responsibilities for data management across the organization?

450
01:00:40.960 --> 01:00:55.910
John Talburt: And what are the resources that you have available for performing data quality management? And then the one that really gets you is how data quality management is applied anywhere the organization is creating or using data.

451
01:00:56.340 --> 01:00:57.859
John Talburt: So this is a big one.

452
01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:00.430
John Talburt: You may think, well.

453
01:01:00.540 --> 01:01:16.809
John Talburt: this is a bank, and I'm producing bank statements, but there may be an administrator or secretary up in the management… in the C-suite working for the CEO or the CMO, and they're putting together a spreadsheet

454
01:01:17.010 --> 01:01:20.449
John Talburt: For somebody to use, that qualifies here.

455
01:01:20.690 --> 01:01:32.700
John Talburt: If you're building a spreadsheet, you're creating or using data, are you applying these data management principles, and does that come under your study that you're documenting?

456
01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:37.110
John Talburt: So this is, one of the requirements to comply.

457
01:01:38.750 --> 01:01:45.850
John Talburt: So that brings us to assessing, and again, as I just… Alright, pointed out.

458
01:01:46.340 --> 01:01:51.980
John Talburt: it doesn't make sense to talk about data quality assessment

459
01:01:52.840 --> 01:01:56.069
John Talburt: If you don't have data quality requirements.

460
01:01:56.950 --> 01:02:04.580
John Talburt: And sometimes you may have these, you may know what they are if you've really documented.

461
01:02:05.030 --> 01:02:09.250
John Talburt: But oftentimes, you have to develop those yourself.

462
01:02:12.010 --> 01:02:17.110
John Talburt: So, this leads to what I call the data quality assessment triangle.

463
01:02:17.330 --> 01:02:22.439
John Talburt: You have data quality requirements, That leads you to…

464
01:02:22.600 --> 01:02:29.140
John Talburt: profiling or analysis data, like you did in this assignment, I ask you to do is…

465
01:02:29.370 --> 01:02:34.749
John Talburt: an analysis of the movie file, but I didn't give you any documented data requirements.

466
01:02:35.090 --> 01:02:40.339
John Talburt: So all you were doing is just this third part down here, data profiling statistics.

467
01:02:42.250 --> 01:02:45.299
John Talburt: Just looking at the data and seeing what's in there.

468
01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:50.020
John Talburt: Without having the data requirements.

469
01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:59.420
John Talburt: you can't really assess the quality. And again, since we… I didn't give you any process, you're not looking at a process to build the product.

470
01:02:59.680 --> 01:03:02.209
John Talburt: You can't really look at the data architecture.

471
01:03:02.760 --> 01:03:10.700
John Talburt: So, to do a full data quality assessment, you really need all three of these things. You need documented data requirements.

472
01:03:11.080 --> 01:03:18.980
John Talburt: You need to look at the data to see what's in there, but you also need to look at the architecture and the process that's being used to build the product.

473
01:03:24.060 --> 01:03:27.949
John Talburt: So, that's what we call data quality assessment.

474
01:03:28.380 --> 01:03:32.040
John Talburt: To add to it the information quality.

475
01:03:32.260 --> 01:03:48.459
John Talburt: assessment. You really need to understand how the business is using these products, and what is the value they're producing, because remember, our definition of information quality is maximizing the value and minimizing the risk.

476
01:03:48.750 --> 01:03:52.900
John Talburt: Of the information that you have in your organization.

477
01:03:53.200 --> 01:04:05.120
John Talburt: Right? And also, for the information products that you're building, are you creating value for the users? So, the IQ assessment is based on

478
01:04:05.230 --> 01:04:07.180
John Talburt: the value proposition

479
01:04:07.520 --> 01:04:14.530
John Talburt: And that should be directly related to the data quality, which is just being sure that you're building things correctly.

480
01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:21.069
John Talburt: It doesn't answer the question, am I building the right thing? Am I building what the consumer wants?

481
01:04:21.310 --> 01:04:32.179
John Talburt: If I'm building what the consumer wants, then I can back into the data quality and say, alright, so what do I need to build that quality product for that consumer?

482
01:04:33.900 --> 01:04:38.820
John Talburt: So that becomes the information quality. I have to add on the value proposition.

483
01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:49.619
John Talburt: I can be very busy building products, but if they're products that nobody likes or uses or complains about, they're not quality.

484
